# The build context is an allowlist: only what is named here reaches the Docker build, so nothing a
# developer or a CI job left in the checkout (an .env, an .npmrc with a token, a key) can end up in
# an image layer. The images are built from the root manifests, the packages, the one script
# @maka/core's build runs (deploy/Dockerfile) and deploy/Caddyfile (the proxy stage).
*
!package.json
!package-lock.json
!tsconfig.base.json
!tsconfig.lib.json
!packages
# @maka/core's prebuild (npm run sync:model-metadata) reads these.
!scripts/sync-model-metadata.mjs
!scripts/model-metadata
!deploy/Caddyfile

# Inside what is allowed, build output stays out (it is rebuilt) ...
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/*.tsbuildinfo
packages/cloud-web/playwright-report
packages/cloud-web/test-results
# A repository nested in an allowed directory carries remote URLs (with credentials) and history.
**/.git

# ... and so do settings and credentials, wherever they sit: environment files, package manager and
# netrc credentials, private keys and certificates, key stores, cloud and SSH directories.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.env
**/.npmrc
**/.yarnrc
**/.yarnrc.yml
**/.pnpmrc
**/.netrc
**/.pypirc
**/.git-credentials
**/.aws
**/.ssh
**/.gnupg
**/.docker
**/.kube
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/*.kdbx
**/id_rsa*
**/id_dsa*
**/id_ecdsa*
**/id_ed25519*
**/service-account*.json
**/credentials.json
